### What is Optional?

Optional<T> is a container introduced in Java 8 that may or may not hold a non-null value. It forces callers to handle absence explicitly.

### How do you create an Optional?

```java
package com.bank.service;

import java.math.BigDecimal;
import java.util.Optional;

public class OptionalCreation {
    record Account(String id, String holder, BigDecimal balance) {}

    public static void main(String[] args) {
        // of: wraps non-null (throws NPE if null)
        Optional<Account> present = Optional.of(
            new Account("SAV2001", "Rajesh", new BigDecimal("125000")));

        // ofNullable: wraps potentially null (null -> empty)
        Account fromDb = null; // simulating not found
        Optional<Account> maybe = Optional.ofNullable(fromDb);

        // empty: explicit absence
        Optional<Account> absent = Optional.empty();

        System.out.println("Present: " + present.isPresent());
        System.out.println("Maybe: " + maybe.isPresent());
        System.out.println("Absent: " + absent.isPresent());
    }
}
```

```text
Present: true
Maybe: false
Absent: false
```

### How do you consume an Optional safely?

```java
package com.bank.service;

import java.math.BigDecimal;
import java.util.Optional;

public class OptionalConsumption {
    record Account(String id, String holder, BigDecimal balance) {}

    static Optional<Account> findAccount(String id) {
        if ("SAV2001".equals(id)) {
            return Optional.of(new Account("SAV2001", "Rajesh", new BigDecimal("125000")));
        }
        return Optional.empty();
    }

    public static void main(String[] args) {
        // orElse: default value
        String name = findAccount("SAV9999")
            .map(Account::holder)
            .orElse("Unknown Customer");
        System.out.println("Name: " + name);

        // orElseThrow: fail fast
        try {
            Account acc = findAccount("SAV9999")
                .orElseThrow(() -> new RuntimeException("Account not found"));
        } catch (RuntimeException e) {
            System.out.println("Error: " + e.getMessage());
        }

        // ifPresent: action only if present
        findAccount("SAV2001")
            .ifPresent(a -> System.out.println("Found: " + a.holder()));

        // map chain: null-safe transformation
        BigDecimal balance = findAccount("SAV2001")
            .map(Account::balance)
            .orElse(BigDecimal.ZERO);
        System.out.println("Balance: Rs." + balance);
    }
}
```

```text
Name: Unknown Customer
Error: Account not found
Found: Rajesh
Balance: Rs.125000
```

### How is Optional different from null checks?

| Aspect | Null return | Optional return |
|--------|------------|----------------|
| Type signal | None: T could be null | Explicit: Optional<T> |
| Caller awareness | Must remember to check | Type forces handling |
| Chaining | Nested if-null checks | map().filter().orElse() |
| NPE risk | High (forget one check) | Low (type system guides) |

### What are common mistakes?

```java
// WRONG: .get() without checking (defeats the purpose!)
// Account a = findAccount("SAV9999").get(); // NoSuchElementException!

// WRONG: isPresent() + get() (glorified null check)
// if (opt.isPresent()) { opt.get().doSomething(); }

// CORRECT: use functional API
findAccount("SAV2001")
    .map(Account::balance)
    .ifPresent(b -> System.out.println("Balance: Rs." + b));
```

### What should a senior engineer know?

Optional is designed for method return types only, not fields, parameters, or collections. It is not Serializable. The functional API (map, flatMap, filter, orElse) is the idiomatic way to use Optional. isPresent+get is an anti-pattern.
